{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/materials/b1-cologne-today-the-cathedral-city-on-the-rhine/audio/main-7e3dc07b795b.mp3","durationSec":391.608,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":4.52,"text":"Cologne is a large German city on the Rhine, the river that runs through it."},{"start":4.52,"end":6.34,"text":"Köln is its German name."},{"start":7.28,"end":10.8,"text":"With over 1 million people, Cologne is Germany’s fourth-largest city."},{"start":11.56,"end":16.42,"text":"It is also the largest city in North Rhine-Westphalia, the German state where it stands."},{"start":17.26,"end":22,"text":"The state capital is Düsseldorf, about 35 kilometres northwest of Cologne."},{"start":23,"end":25.5,"text":"Most of Cologne lies on the left bank of the Rhine."},{"start":26.3,"end":31.22,"text":"The river is an important part of the city’s setting, but Cologne offers more than river views."},{"start":32,"end":34.62,"text":"It has more than 30 museums and hundreds of galleries."},{"start":35.42,"end":39.82,"text":"Its mix of older places and newer buildings gives the city a varied appearance."},{"start":41.36,"end":47.86,"text":"The Romans founded Colonia Claudia Ara Agrippinensium, the Roman settlement here, in AD 50."},{"start":48.64,"end":54.66,"text":"They named it after Emperor Claudius, a Roman ruler, and his wife Agrippina, who was born here."},{"start":55.02,"end":57.18,"text":"The name Cologne comes from Colonia."},{"start":57.96,"end":61.52,"text":"In the Middle Ages, Cologne was a rich trade city on the Rhine."},{"start":62.12,"end":65.14,"text":"It joined the Hanseatic League, a group of trading cities."},{"start":66.32,"end":69.22,"text":"The river connected the city with other places and their goods."},{"start":70.04,"end":76.5,"text":"Much later, bombing during the Second World War destroyed around 80% of the old city centre."},{"start":76.5,"end":78.3,"text":"Cologne was rebuilt after the war."},{"start":79.2,"end":84.82,"text":"Today, Roman remains and medieval churches stand alongside many newer buildings from the rebuilding."},{"start":85.66,"end":89.24,"text":"The centre is not simply an old city preserved unchanged."},{"start":90.46,"end":95.58,"text":"Cologne Cathedral, also called Kölner Dom, is a Catholic church in the Gothic style."},{"start":96.8,"end":103.68,"text":"Building began in 1248, stopped around 1560, and finished in 1880 according to the medieval plan."},{"start":104.56,"end":108.92,"text":"The full span was 632 years, including the long pause."},{"start":109.52,"end":116.04,"text":"Its two spires are 157 metres tall, making it the world’s tallest twin-spired church."},{"start":117.06,"end":121.5,"text":"It was built to hold the Shrine of the Three Kings, a golden medieval shrine."},{"start":122.76,"end":128.72,"text":"In 1996, it became a UNESCO World Heritage Site, a place recognized for its special cultural value."},{"start":129.38,"end":134.18,"text":"It is Germany’s most visited landmark, with about 6 million visitors a year."},{"start":134.18,"end":139.54,"text":"Fourteen bombs hit it during the Second World War, but it stayed standing."},{"start":140.42,"end":147.76,"text":"Visitors can climb 533 steps in the South Tower to a viewing platform about 100 metres high."},{"start":148.8,"end":150.72,"text":"The route passes the bell chamber."},{"start":151.9,"end":155.94,"text":"Since 1 July 2026, tourist visits inside require a ticket."},{"start":156.74,"end":161.7,"text":"People coming to pray can still enter free through the entrance facing Cologne Central Station."},{"start":162.16,"end":166.88,"text":"The Hohenzollern Bridge is a railway bridge across the Rhine next to the cathedral."},{"start":167.39,"end":172.7,"text":"Built from 1907 to 1911, it carries more than 1,200 trains a day."},{"start":173.48,"end":175.68,"text":"It is Germany’s busiest railway bridge."},{"start":176.6,"end":182.6,"text":"Since 2008, couples have attached love locks, padlocks that express their love, to its fence."},{"start":183.84,"end":191.1,"text":"The Old Town, Cologne’s historic central area, includes the squares Alter Markt and Heumarkt near the Rhine."},{"start":191.76,"end":195.3,"text":"A riverside walk offers a different way to enjoy this part of the city."},{"start":196.18,"end":202.9,"text":"At Rheinauhafen, a harbour on the Rhine, the Chocolate Museum follows 5,000 years of chocolate history."},{"start":203.72,"end":211.14,"text":"Visitors can see cocoa plants in a 10-metre-high tropical house, watch chocolate being made, and enjoy the café’s river view."},{"start":211.7,"end":217.6,"text":"Museum Ludwig, a modern art museum next to the cathedral, has a building that opened in 1986."},{"start":218.64,"end":225.36,"text":"It has Pop Art, art connected with popular culture, and around 900 works by the artist Pablo Picasso."},{"start":226.2,"end":231.34,"text":"Its Picasso collection is the world’s third-largest, after Barcelona and Paris."},{"start":231.34,"end":234.7,"text":"Kölsch is Cologne’s local beer."},{"start":235.48,"end":243.44,"text":"Its name has been protected in the European Union since 1997, and real Kölsch must be brewed within about 50 kilometres of Cologne."},{"start":244.56,"end":249.1,"text":"It comes in a Stange, a tall, thin 200 ml glass."},{"start":249.9,"end":253.82,"text":"A Köbes, the waiter, carries glasses on a round tray."},{"start":254.34,"end":260.36,"text":"The waiter brings another when yours is empty and marks each drink on your beer mat, the small mat under your glass."},{"start":261.24,"end":263.44,"text":"Putting the mat on top means you want to stop."},{"start":264.6,"end":268.64,"text":"Cologne Carnival is called the fifth season, the city’s special carnival period."},{"start":269.36,"end":271.26,"text":"It opens at 11:11 on 11 November."},{"start":272.82,"end":279.28,"text":"Street carnival runs from Weiberfastnacht, the Thursday before Lent, to Ash Wednesday, the start of Lent."},{"start":280.08,"end":283.7,"text":"Its highlight is the Rose Monday parade, two days before Ash Wednesday."},{"start":285.02,"end":288.3,"text":"The local greeting is Kölle Alaaf."},{"start":288.3,"end":289.98,"text":"Work also brings people together."},{"start":289.98,"end":295.1,"text":"Koelnmesse, Cologne’s trade fair centre, holds around 80 trade fairs a year."},{"start":295.8,"end":299.96,"text":"A trade fair brings businesses and visitors together around a subject or industry."},{"start":300.96,"end":303.44,"text":"One example is gamescom, the video game fair."},{"start":304.78,"end":309.44,"text":"RTL, Germany’s largest private broadcaster, also has its headquarters here."},{"start":310.04,"end":312.84,"text":"Cologne opened its first Christmas market in 1820."},{"start":313.5,"end":317.76,"text":"Today, its Christmas markets attract about 4 million visitors a year."},{"start":318.5,"end":322.9,"text":"These seasonal markets sell food, gifts, and decorations among Christmas lights."},{"start":323.98,"end":328.34,"text":"The Market of Hearts is on Roncalliplatz, the square in front of the cathedral."},{"start":328.88,"end":337.98,"text":"In 2026, it runs from 16 November to 23 December, with more than 140 stalls and a stage beneath a tent of lights."},{"start":340.12,"end":345.3,"text":"The Market of Angels is on Neumarkt, a central square near the main shopping streets."},{"start":345.3,"end":350.58,"text":"Stars shine in the trees, and people dressed as angels walk through the market."},{"start":350.58,"end":359.05,"text":"Heinzels Wintermärchen, another market, spreads across Alter Markt and Heumarkt."},{"start":359.05,"end":364.91,"text":"It takes its name from the Heinzelmännchen, little house elves in a Cologne legend."},{"start":365.49,"end":373.49,"text":"It opens on 23 November 2026, and its Heumarkt area stays open until 10 January 2027."},{"start":374.23,"end":380.47,"text":"There is a large open-air ice rink for skating on Heumarkt and a Christmas Ferris wheel at the Chocolate Museum."},{"start":380.47,"end":385.65,"text":"Visitors can enjoy waffles and Glühwein, hot red wine with spices."},{"start":386.53,"end":390.99,"text":"The Christmas Market Express, a small train, takes people between markets."}],"words":[{"text":"Cologne","start":0,"end":0.34},{"text":"is","start":0.34,"end":0.54},{"text":"a","start":0.54,"end":0.68},{"text":"large","start":0.68,"end":1.04},{"text":"German","start":1.04,"end":1.46},{"text":"city","start":1.46,"end":1.86},{"text":"on","start":1.86,"end":2.14},{"text":"the","start":2.14,"end":2.3},{"text":"Rhine","start":2.3,"end":2.62},{"text":"the","start":3.14,"end":3.18},{"text":"river","start":3.18,"end":3.38},{"text":"that","start":3.38,"end":3.6},{"text":"runs","start":3.6,"end":3.82},{"text":"through","start":3.82,"end":4.12},{"text":"it","start":4.12,"end":4.52},{"text":"Köln","start":4.52,"end":5.22},{"text":"is","start":5.22,"end":5.52},{"text":"its","start":5.52,"end":5.7},{"text":"German","start":5.7,"end":6},{"text":"name","start":6,"end":6.34},{"text":"With","start":7.28,"end":7.38},{"text":"over","start":7.38,"end":7.62},{"text":"1","start":7.62,"end":7.84},{"text":"million","start":7.84,"end":8.08},{"text":"people","start":8.08,"end":8.56},{"text":"Cologne","start":8.98,"end":9.1},{"text":"is","start":9.1,"end":9.32},{"text":"Germany’s","start":9.32,"end":9.82},{"text":"fourth-largest","start":9.82,"end":10.44},{"text":"city","start":10.44,"end":10.8},{"text":"It","start":11.56,"end":11.7},{"text":"is","start":11.7,"end":11.8},{"text":"also","start":11.8,"end":12.06},{"text":"the","start":12.06,"end":12.24},{"text":"largest","start":12.24,"end":12.52},{"text":"city","start":12.52,"end":12.84},{"text":"in","start":12.84,"end":13.12},{"text":"North","start":13.12,"end":13.4},{"text":"Rhine-Westphalia","start":13.4,"end":14.8},{"text":"the","start":14.8,"end":14.98},{"text":"German","start":14.98,"end":15.28},{"text":"state","start":15.28,"end":15.62},{"text":"where","start":15.62,"end":15.88},{"text